"A long key protects the message better.": "049b4c71d03b",
"Caesar’s lock has a weakness. There are only 25 possible keys. A watcher can simply try them all, one after another, until the message makes sense. So the safety of a lock depends on how many keys there are. A computer’s key is a number written in bits, and you saw in Lesson 1 that every extra bit doubles the count. A standard method used today has keys of 128, 192, or 256 bits. A key of 128 bits can be any one of a number of keys that is 39 digits long.": "03a6e9a87b97",
"Can you keep a secret?": "a9296cb46f4c",
"Here is one way, used by Julius Caesar. Move every letter three places along the alphabet: A becomes D, B becomes E, and so on. “MEET ME AT NOON” turns into “PHHW PH DW QRRQ.” Scrambling a message like this is called encryption. There are two parts to it. The rule is “move each letter along.” The key is the number that says how far: here, 3.": "d10ee6ebea6e",
"In Caesar’s cipher, what is the rule and what is the key? Which one must be kept secret?": "8ca5dee38ef7",
"In Lesson 13 you saw that a message does not go straight from your computer to the other one. It passes through many machines on the way, and you do not control any of them. Anyone who runs one of those machines could look at what goes by. So how can you send something private? The answer is very old. It is called cryptography: writing a message so that only the person it is meant for can understand it.": "b3936747bc8a",
"In Lesson 13 you saw that a message does not go straight from your computer to the other one. It passes through many machines on the way, and you do not control any of them. Anyone who runs one of those machines could look at what goes by. So how can you send something private? The answer is very old. It is called cryptography: writing a message so that only the person it is meant for can understand it. Here is one way, used by Julius Caesar. Move every letter three places along the alphabet: A becomes D, B becomes E, and so on. “MEET ME AT NOON” turns into “PHHW PH DW QRRQ.” Scrambling a message like this is called encryption. There are two parts to it. The rule is “move each letter along.” The key is the number that says how far: here, 3. Your friend knows the key, so your friend moves every letter back three places and reads the message. That is decryption. The watcher in the middle sees the same letters, but without the key they mean nothing. Notice what has to be secret. It is not the rule. Everyone can know the rule. Only the key must be kept between the two of you, and you must both have exactly the same one. Caesar’s lock has a weakness. There are only 25 possible keys. A watcher can simply try them all, one after another, until the message makes sense. So the safety of a lock depends on how many keys there are. A computer’s key is a number written in bits, and you saw in Lesson 1 that every extra bit doubles the count. A standard method used today has keys of 128, 192, or 256 bits. A key of 128 bits can be any one of a number of keys that is 39 digits long. One puzzle remains. How can two computers that have never met agree on a key, when the watcher hears everything they say? In 1976, two researchers, Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman, described a way. Each side has two keys. One is published for everyone to use for locking. The other is kept secret and is the only one that unlocks. Anyone can send you a locked message, and only you can open it. You use all this every day. When a web address begins with https, the browser and the server are encrypting everything they send to each other. The machines in between see only scrambled data. But remember what encryption does and does not do. It keeps the message private on the way. It cannot tell you whether the person at the other end is honest, and it cannot help if you give your password to the wrong person.": "d31795e26b6c",
